DECIDED with the maintainer 2026-08-28, after looking at ../io-k8s-p5, which
solves the same problem for Kubernetes. Read that project before starting:
it is the working reference, not an analogy.

WHAT IS BEING BUILT

A typed object model over the Docker Engine API, generated from Docker's own
swagger, replacing the seven hand-written entity classes.

  $container->State->Running          instead of  $container->{State}{Running}
  ->create(host_config => { port_bindings => ... })  reaching the daemon as
                                       HostConfig => { PortBindings => ... }

THE DECISIONS ALREADY MADE -- do not re-litigate these

1. GENERATED CLASSES REPLACE THE ENTITY CLASSES. API::Docker::Container,
   ::Image, ::Network, ::Volume, ::Plugin, ::Secret and ::Config go away in
   their present hand-written form. Their convenience methods (->remove,
   ->logs, ->attach, ->decoded_data ...) survive on top of the generated
   classes. One model, not two. Nothing has been released, so this breaks
   nobody.

2. VERSION AWARENESS IS POD ONLY. Every attribute records in its POD which
   API version introduced it. Nothing is checked, warned about or dropped at
   runtime: the field goes out and the engine ignores what it does not know,
   exactly as today. Reasons: we measured repeatedly that Podman serves
   fields its announced version does not promise and vice versa, so we are
   not the authority on what an engine can do; and silently dropping a field
   the caller set is the class of bug this distribution spent a whole day
   removing. The registry makes runtime checking retrofittable if it is ever
   wanted.

3. APPROACH C: build it in one round rather than a core-first subset. The
   maintainer chose this over the safer staged option. It is only safe if
   the DSL and the pattern are settled BEFORE the mass of classes is written
   -- see the skill.

MEASURED FACTS (2026-08-28, so nobody has to re-derive them)

  Docker publishes every API version as swagger:
    https://docs.docker.com/reference/api/engine/version/v1.51.yaml
    v1.41, v1.44, v1.48, v1.51 all answer 200. v1.51 is 458 KB.

  Definitions per version:  v1.41: 104   v1.44: 114   v1.51: 132

  Of the 102 definitions v1.41 and v1.51 share, only 19 have changed fields
  at all: 24 fields added, 31 removed. The version differences are local,
  not pervasive. Biggest movers: OCIDescriptor (+5), SystemInfo (+4),
  ImageInspect (+2), ImageSummary (+2), EndpointSettings (+2).

  The swagger carries NO per-field version information. "Since 1.48" can only
  be derived by diffing two specs against each other -- which is what
  io-k8s-p5's maint/spec-drift-check.pl --from/--to already does.

  A real `container inspect` from Docker 29.7.2 has 134 distinct keys at
  depth 4. That is why this is generated and not typed by hand.

  This machine: Podman 5.8.4 announces API 1.44, Docker 29.7.2 announces
  1.55. The newest published swagger is 1.51. Generate against 1.51.

WHAT TO COPY FROM ../io-k8s-p5

  lib/IO/K8s/Resource.pm      the DSL. `k8s name => Type;` writes into an
                              attribute registry that both serialisation and
                              the drift checker read. This is the load-
                              bearing idea -- not the classes.
  maint/spec-drift-check.pl   diffs the upstream spec against the registry,
                              field by field, with an exceptions file for
                              deliberate deviations. Two modes: coverage
                              (spec vs lib/) and compare (spec vs spec).
  spec/v1.36.3.json           the spec lives in the repo.
  the class shape             `k8s field => Type;` immediately followed by
                              =attr POD taken from the spec's description.

ORDER OF WORK

  1. spec/ with v1.51 (and v1.41 + v1.44 for the diff)
  2. API::Docker::Type -- the DSL, the registry, serialisation both ways
  3. THREE classes by hand, covering the three shapes: flat (Port), nested
     (HostConfig), array-of-objects (Mount)
  4. maint/spec-drift-check.pl, run against those three
  5. only then the remaining ~129, in parallel
  6. the entity classes rebuilt on top, keeping their convenience methods
  7. the resource classes returning them

Steps 2-4 are what makes approach C survivable: if the pattern is wrong,
it is wrong in three classes rather than 132.

SEE ALSO the skill api-docker-type-model, which carries the pattern, and the
api-docker-type-writer agent, which is briefed with it.


DECIDED 2026-08-28, maintainer, on top of the above:

STEP 5 IS GENERATED, AND THE GENERATOR MAY ONLY EVER CREATE.

maint/spec-to-type.pl writes the ~129 remaining classes out of
spec/v1.51.yaml. The three hand-written classes from step 3 are its
acceptance test: the generator must reproduce them byte for byte.

But: THE GENERATOR IS NEVER A CLASS UPDATER. It may write a file that does
not exist yet. It must refuse to overwrite one that does -- no --force, no
"regenerate all". When a newer spec appears, the drift checker reports the
difference and a human or an agent decides field by field what happens; a
generated class is a class someone has since read, corrected and documented,
and a re-run would silently throw that away.

Enforce it in the script itself, not only in prose: an existing file means
refuse, or write to a staging path for diffing. Anything that reads like a
bulk refresh is the failure mode being ruled out here.


